# ── Everything goes through the API gateway ──────────────────────────────────
#
# Deployed: vercel.json rewrites the relative paths this app calls to the
# gateway, which authenticates, meters and routes them to the real service.
# The browser only ever talks to this app's own origin, so CORS never applies.
#
#     https://api-gateway-two-nu.vercel.app
#
# That is why the VITE_* base URLs below stay EMPTY. Setting one bakes an
# absolute URL into the bundle, so the browser calls that host directly and
# bypasses the gateway entirely — no auth enforcement, no metering, no budgets,
# and the target service then needs this app's origin in its CORS allowlist.
# Leave them unset unless you are deliberately bypassing the gateway.
#
# Local dev: the Vite proxy in vite.config.ts forwards the same paths. Point it
# at a locally running gateway (port 8000) to exercise the same path, or at the
# individual services to work on one in isolation.

# cp .env.example .env
#
# Which auth-service this console talks to. The app always calls RELATIVE
# paths (/auth/...), and something in front rewrites them to the real service:
#
#   local dev   the Vite dev-server proxy   -> AUTH_PROXY_TARGET below
#   deployed    the Vercel rewrite          -> vercel.json
#
# Both hops are server-side, so the browser is always same-origin and the
# auth-service CORS allowlist never comes into it.

# ── Local development ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where `npm run dev` forwards /auth/* . Defaults to a local API GATEWAY on
# :8000, which routes /auth to auth-service — the gateway is the only thing on
# the platform that talks to auth-service, and dev goes through it for the same
# reason production does. Point it at a deployed gateway to work against real
# accounts without running anything locally:
#
#   GATEWAY_PROXY_TARGET=https://api-gateway-two-nu.vercel.app
#
# AUTH_PROXY_TARGET is still read as a fallback so an existing .env keeps
# working, but it now names a gateway rather than auth-service itself.
GATEWAY_PROXY_TARGET=

# ── Escape hatch ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bakes an absolute base URL into the bundle, so the BROWSER calls that host
# directly, bypassing both proxies above. You then MUST add this app's origin
# to the target's AUTHSVC_CORS_ORIGINS — including every Vercel preview URL,
# which change per deployment. Prefer the vercel.json rewrite; only reach for
# this when the console is hosted somewhere that can't rewrite.
VITE_AUTH_BASE_URL=

# NOTE: whichever you point at, this console administers that instance's REAL
# accounts — create/rename/delete take effect there.
